course: world
lesson: 91
title: Being a scientist — fair tests, guesses and evidence
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A scientist starts with a question, makes a careful guess called a prediction, and then tests it to see whether the world agrees.
explain: In a fair test you change only one thing and keep everything else the same, so that if the result changes, you know which thing caused it.
explain: You measure the results, repeat the test several times and look at all the evidence, even the results you did not expect.
explain: Two things happening together does not prove that one causes the other: more ice creams and more sunburns both happen in summer, but ice cream does not cause sunburn — the sunshine causes both.
explain: Example: to find out whether plants grow better with plant food, you grow two groups of the same plants in the same pots, soil, light and water, and give plant food to only one group.
fact: A prediction is a guess about what will happen, based on what you already know.
fact: In a fair test only one thing is changed and everything else is kept the same.
fact: The thing you change on purpose is called the variable you are testing.
fact: A control group is kept without the change so you can compare the results with it.
fact: Repeating a test makes the result more trustworthy.
fact: Testing many plants or people is better than testing just one.
fact: Evidence is what you observe and measure, not what you hope or believe.
fact: A wrong prediction is still useful, because it teaches you something.
fact: Two things happening together does not prove that one causes the other.
fact: Scientists change their minds when good new evidence shows they were wrong.
fact: Scientists write down their method so that others can repeat the test and check it.
exercise: What is a prediction? | A careful guess about what will happen | A result you have already measured in a test | A rule that is always true, everywhere | A special tool for measuring how far things go
exercise: In a fair test, how many things do you change on purpose? | Only one | Two at once | As many as you can | None at all
exercise: Why should you change only one thing in a test? | So you know what caused any change in the result | So the test takes less time | So the result is always the one you wanted | So you need fewer tools
exercise: What is a control group for? | To compare with the group that was changed | To make the whole test go much faster and easier | To replace a broken experiment | To check the measuring tools
exercise: Why do scientists repeat their tests? | To make sure the result was not luck or a mistake | To make the result bigger | Because the first result is always wrong | To use up their materials
exercise: Which is evidence? | A measurement taken during a test | What you hoped would happen in the test | What your best friend believes is true | A guess made before the test has even started
exercise: Your prediction turns out to be wrong. What should you do? | Report it honestly and learn from it | Change the numbers so they fit the guess | Hide the result | Say the test never happened
exercise: Why is testing 20 plants better than testing 1? | One plant might be unusual by chance | Twenty plants always grow faster than one | One plant on its own cannot be measured | Twenty plants need less water than one plant
exercise: Why do scientists write down exactly what they did? | So others can repeat it and check it | So they can sell the paper for money later | So nobody else is able to copy them | So the result will change the next time
exercise: More ice creams are sold on days with more sunburns. What causes both? | Hot sunny weather | Ice cream causes sunburn | Sunburn makes people hungry | Shops cause sunburn
exercise: What do good scientists do when new evidence shows they were wrong? | Change their minds | Ignore the evidence | Argue louder and longer | Stop doing science for good
exercise: You test whether a ball bounces higher on wood or carpet. What must stay the same? | The ball and the height you drop it from | The floor you drop it on | Nothing at all needs to stay the same each time | Only the colour of the ball
exercise: Which question can be answered with a fair test? | Does salt make ice melt faster? | Which colour is the most beautiful? | Is my dog happier than your cat? | What will I dream tonight?
test: Lily tests two paper planes, but one is bigger and she throws it harder. Why is this not a fair test? | Two things were changed, so she cannot tell which one mattered | She should have used three planes | Paper planes cannot be tested | She threw them on the same day
test: A boy wears his lucky socks and wins a race. He says the socks made him win. What is wrong with his thinking? | One win together with the socks does not prove the socks caused it | Socks always make you slower | He should have measured the socks | Lucky things only work on Fridays
test: Ana tests a new plant food on one plant, which grows well. What would make her result more convincing? | Testing many plants, with some getting no plant food | Testing the same single plant again every month next year | Giving the plant twice as much food | Telling everyone that it works
test: Which is the best reason to trust a scientific result? | Other people repeated the test and got the same result | A famous person said it was true | Many people would really like it to be true, so it must be | It was written in big letters
test: Ben predicts that heavier balls fall faster. He drops a heavy and a light ball of the same size and they land together. What should he conclude? | His prediction was wrong for these balls | His test must be broken | The light ball must have been pushed | He should keep only the heavy ball result
test: To test whether sugar dissolves faster in hot water, what is the fair way? | Same amount of sugar and water, change only the temperature | Hot water with more sugar and cold water with less | Stir only the cold cup | Use a big cup for the hot water and a small cup for the cold
test: In a town, children who own more books get better marks. What could explain this other than the books themselves? | Something else, like families who read together, causes both | Books always make children clever by themselves | Good marks make books appear at home | Books are given out as prizes for marks only
test: Two pupils measure the same jump and get very different numbers. What should they do first? | Measure again carefully and check the method | Choose the number they like best | Add the numbers and stop | Throw away both numbers for ever and pick a new jump
test: Why does a scientist keep a control group in a medicine test? | To see what would happen without the medicine | To give the medicine to more and more people at once | To make the test cheaper | Because the medicine needs company
test: my bean plant grew loads when i sang to it, so singing works? | Not proven; test many plants and keep a control without singing | Yes, one plant is enough proof | Yes, plants always grow way more with music, everyone knows that | No, plants can never be tested
test: gonna test which paper towel soaks up most, what do i keep the same | Everything except the kind of paper towel | Nothing, just change everything at once and see | Only the colour of the paper towels | Only the time of day you test
test: Mia says if ur prediction is wrong the experiment failed. true? | No, a wrong prediction is still useful, because it teaches you something | Yes, a wrong prediction makes the test useless | Yes, you must change the results to match | No, but you must never write it down
test: a video said ice cream sales and sunburn go up together, so ice cream gives u sunburn. right? | No, two things happening together does not prove one causes the other | Yes, ice cream makes skin burn faster | Yes, because both happen in summer | No, sunburn makes people buy ice cream
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a fair test? | Only one thing is changed and everything else is kept the same | Every condition is changed at the same time | Nothing whatsoever is changed | The result is decided before the test
exam: Identify the purpose of a control group in an experiment. | It is kept without the change so the results may be compared | It receives the greatest amount of the change under examination | It decides the prediction to be tested | It records the results on behalf of others
exam: It is given that two things are observed to occur together. Determine the conclusion which may properly be drawn. | It is not thereby proved that one causes the other | It is thereby proved that the first causes the second | It is thereby proved that the second causes the first | It is thereby proved that neither has any cause
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct of scientists confronted with good new evidence. | They change their minds when it shows them to be wrong | They disregard it in favour of their earlier, firmer beliefs | They conceal it from other scientists | They repeat their former claims more firmly
